  "nameStory": "This mushroom gets its common name from mycologist Charles Horton Peck, a pioneering 19th-century botanist who described many North American fungi. The 'yellow dust' part refers to the powdery yellow remnants of the universal veil that often coat the cap and stem, giving it a dusty, golden appearance.",
  "description": "Peck's Yellow Dust Amanita is a medium-sized woodland mushroom with a distinctive dusty yellow coating on its cap and stem. Like all amanitas, it emerges from an egg-like structure and has white gills that don't attach to the stem. The mushroom typically reaches 3-6 inches tall with a cap that starts rounded and flattens with age. It's part of the large and diverse amanita family, which includes both deadly poisonous and edible species.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap",
      "description": "Yellow to golden-brown with a powdery, dusty coating of yellowish patches that can be brushed off. The cap starts dome-shaped and flattens as it matures, feeling smooth underneath the dusty coating.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Gills",
      "description": "Pure white and free from the stem, meaning they don't touch or attach to it. They remain white throughout the mushroom's life and feel papery to the touch.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem",
      "description": "White to pale yellow with a bulbous base that sits in a cup-like volva. The stem often has yellowish dusty patches similar to the cap and feels firm when fresh.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Spore Print",
      "description": "White spore print when you place the cap gill-side down on dark paper overnight. This white print is characteristic of all amanitas.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Smell",
      "description": "Mild, pleasant mushroomy odor without any strong chemical or unpleasant notes. Some detect a faintly sweet or earthy scent.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Found in mixed hardwood and coniferous forests throughout New England, forming beneficial partnerships with tree roots. Prefers well-drained soils under oaks, maples, and pines. Most commonly spotted along forest edges and in mature woodlands with rich leaf litter.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Rarely found in spring. Mycelium is active underground but fruiting bodies don't typically appear until warmer weather arrives.",
    "summer": "Peak fruiting season from July through August, especially after warm summer rains. Look for fresh specimens emerging from the forest floor during humid periods.",
    "fall": "Continues fruiting into early fall, particularly in September. Later specimens may have weathered caps with less prominent yellow dusty coating.",
    "winter": "No fruiting bodies present. The underground mycelial network remains dormant beneath the forest floor until the following growing season."
  },
  "funFact": "The yellow dusty coating that gives this mushroom its name is actually the remains of a protective 'egg shell' that completely enclosed the baby mushroom - as it grows, this universal veil breaks apart and leaves behind the characteristic golden patches like remnants of a burst balloon.",
  "confusionNotes": "Most likely confused with Yellow Patches (Amanita flavoconia), which has more orange-yellow coloring and smaller stature. Remember: Peck's is generally larger and more golden-yellow, while Yellow Patches tends toward orange tones and grows smaller. Both have the telltale white gills and bulbous stem base of amanitas.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "Charles Horton Peck described over 2,700 species of fungi during his career as New York State Botanist, making him one of America's most prolific mycologists. Many mushrooms bear his name, but this 'elongata' species specifically honors his work documenting northeastern fungi in the late 1800s.",
  "compareHints": "Unlike the deadly Deathcap which has a smooth white cap, Peck's Yellow Dust Amanita has distinctive yellow powdery patches. It's smaller than the common Blusher and lacks that species' reddish bruising reaction. The yellow coloring easily separates it from white amanitas like the Destroying Angel, though all amanitas should be treated with extreme caution regarding edibility.",
